  "text_md": "Cotgravp, in v.\nfor conveying water. Var. dial.\nHawkins, iii. 357\nEsguiere ,' Leg. Cathol. p. 154 ;\nGuy of Warwick, Middlehill MS. LAVEROCK. The lark. North. See Wright's\n;\n# LAV 508 LAW\nlawnd,\" Nominale MS.\nLAVEER. To work a ship against the wind.\n;\nLAU3T. Caught ; received. {A.-S.)\nlaverkes, Beves of\n- (4) Low. North. He wist not that hym was gode. But then he putte doune his hode On knees he fel downe latvc.\nAnely lawand thameselfe to the sacramenles of haly kyrke, thof it be swa that thay hafebenecumbyrde in syne and with syne alle thaire lyfe tyme.\nploughed plain ; a park ; a lawn. \" Saltus, a\n![](<11_pg36_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\nYf that y wylle sufifur the To hulde me a lavour and bason to my honde.\nSumlyme unto the laverok.\nLAVENDER. To lay in lavender, to pawn. This is a very common phrase in old plays. \" To lay to pawne, as we say to lay in lavander,\" Florio, p. 27.\nAnd fulleglad, certys, thou schalt bee,\n(2) A cistern, trough, or conduit, to wash in. \" Laver to washe at, lavoyr\" Palsgrave. Also, a basin. See Florio, p. 89 ;\n(2) Lawing of dogs, i. e. cutting out the balls,\ni. 7; Davies' Ancient Rites, 1672, p. 130.\nMS. Lincoln A. 1. 17, f- 229. LAWE. (1) To laugh. Nominale MS.\nLAVISH. Rank, as grass, &c. West.\nReliq. Antiq.\nLAWGHE. Low. Hampole.\n![](<11_pg36_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\nMorte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 53.\nLeave protestations now, and let us hie To tread lavolta, that is women's walk.\nTopsell's Beasts, p. 366 ;\nwindpipe. Still used in Devon.\nLAVER. (1) The remainder. North.\nLAVALTOE. Same as Lavolta, q. v. For lo ! the liveless Jacks lavaltoes take At that sweet musick which themselves do make.\nLAVAS. Lavish, Romeus and Juliet, p. 20.\nLAWING. (1) Going to law. IJnc.\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii.38, f. 144.\nThenne was Marie Joseph bitau5t,\nwaltz, formerly much in fashion. The man turned the woman round several times, and then assisted her in making a high spring.\nLavelugged, Northumb. HoUoway has lapeared in use in Sussex and Hants.\n- (4) To hang, or flap down. Hall. LAVE-EARED. Long, or flap-eared. See\nLyric Poetry, pp. 26, 40 ;\nThat tay after thaym ne went To du thayr laverd comandement.\nSyr, God gyve yow good morow.\nLaundring gold, washing it.\n;\n- (3) Custom ; manner. See Ellis, ii. 335.\nLenges all at laysere, and lokes one the wallys\nLAURER. A laurel. Chaucer.\nLAUNDRE. A laundress. Palsgrave.\nLAUS. Loose. {A.-S.)\nMorte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 80.\n- (3) A dish composed of a kind of sea-weed well washed and boiled. It is also called laver^ bread, Kennett, MS. Lansd. 1033.\nHamtoun, p. 138,\nTyrlery lovpyn, the laverocke songe,\nSo meryly pypes the sparow\nWho lukes to the lefte syde, wheune his horse launches. With the lyghte of the sonne men myghtesee his lyvere.",
